你知道我喜欢坐浴盆。

327
00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:26.240
That's a story for another time.
那是另一个故事了。

328
00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:26.780
It is.
确实。

329
00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.060
I would not want to put snow up there.
我可不想把雪塞进那里。

330
00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:30.740
It'd be very cold.
那会非常冷。

331
00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:31.200
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

332
00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:38.160
In Roman times, they used a sponge attached to a stick.
在罗马时代，他们用绑在棍子上的海绵。

333
00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:39.240
Fancy.
真讲究。

334
00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:40.520
I saw a picture of it.
我见过那东西的照片。

335
00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:42.980
It kind of looked like a loofah on the end of a branch.
看起来就像树枝末端绑了个丝瓜络。

336
00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:44.220
A stinky loofah.
一个臭烘烘的丝瓜络。

337
00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.520
I mean, listen to what they would do.
我是说，听听他们是怎么做的。

338
00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:58.380
They would keep it in a bucket full of vinegar or salt water to sanitize it, and then you would sponge your butt with it after you pooped.
他们会把它放在装满醋或盐水的桶里消毒，然后你拉完屎就用它擦屁股。

339
00:13:59.320 --> 00:14:08.920
Some really rich people would use cloth, but because cloth was handmade at the time, that was considered, like, very decadent if you used cloth.
一些非常有钱的人会用布，但因为当时的布是手工制作的，所以如果用布擦屁股会被认为非常奢侈。

340
00:14:09.280 --> 00:14:17.260
But by the early 14th century, the Chinese were manufacturing toilet paper at around 10 million packages a year.
但到了14世纪初，中国人每年已经能生产约1000万包卫生纸了。

341
00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:19.280
But you know what?
但你知道吗？

342
00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:27.720
The modern toilet paper did not get to the West until 1857, and before that, you know what we were using over here?
现代卫生纸直到1857年才传到西方，在那之前，你知道我们这边用的是什么吗？

343
00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:30.220
Please don't say a shell.
请别说是贝壳。

344
00:14:30.680 --> 00:14:32.240
It was not a shell.
不是贝壳。

345
00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:32.660
Okay.
好吧。

346
00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:34.880
We were using corn cobs.
我们用的是玉米芯。

347
00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:36.780
Corn cobs?
玉米芯？

348
00:14:36.920 --> 00:14:39.380
Or old newspapers or magazines.
或者是旧报纸或杂志。

349
00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:41.160
Yeah.
是的。

350
00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:53.100
The first toilet paper roll was introduced in 1890, and then really the biggest milestone in toilet paper happened in 1930,
第一卷卫生纸是在1890年推出的，而卫生纸历史上真正重大的里程碑发生在1930年，

351
00:14:53.100 --> 00:15:00.240
when Northern Tissue Company made the first splinter-free toilet paper.
当北方纸业公司生产出第一款无木刺的卫生纸时。

352
00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:01.900
Splinters?
木刺？

353
00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:06.920
All this time, people were wiping their butts with paper that had splinters?
这么久以来，人们一直用带木刺的纸擦屁股？

354
00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:09.640
I had to read that several times.
我不得不读了好几遍才敢相信。

355
00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.160
Yeah, but thank you, Northern Tissue Company.
是啊，不过还是要感谢北方纸业公司。

356
00:15:13.380 --> 00:15:14.100
Seriously?
认真的吗？

357
00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:16.600
Splinter-free since 1930.
自1930年起就无木刺了。

358
00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.180
Good night.
晚安。

359
00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.280
Here's something I learned.
我学到了一个知识点。

360
00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:29.780
In 1973, America experienced its first toilet paper shortage, and it was all because of a joke that Johnny Carson made on The Tonight Show.
1973年，美国经历了第一次卫生纸短缺，而这一切都是因为强尼·卡森在《今夜秀》里开的一个玩笑。

361
00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:30.840
What'd he say?
他说了什么？

362
00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:32.920
I have an audio clip.
我有一段音频。

363
00:15:33.080 --> 00:15:33.560
Ooh!
噢！

364
00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:36.700
Of all the shortages we had, there's a gasoline shortage.
在我们经历的所有短缺中，现在有汽油短缺。

365
00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:38.920
You know what else is disappearing from the supermarket shelves?
你们知道还有什么正在从超市货架上消失吗？

366
00:15:40.280 --> 00:15:41.160
Toilet paper.
卫生纸。

367
00:15:41.840 --> 00:15:43.460
Ha ha ha, you can laugh now.
哈哈，你们现在可以笑了。

368
00:15:43.460 --> 00:15:54.140
Now, there is an acute shortage of toilet paper in the good old United States.
现在，我们亲爱的美国正面临严重的卫生纸短缺。

369
00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:55.380
We've got to quit writing on it.
我们得停止在上面写字了。

370
00:15:57.500 --> 00:15:59.120
But I want to tell you, it is serious.
但我得告诉你们，这事儿很严重。

371
00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.720
I just saw a commercial where...
我刚看到一个广告，里面……

372
00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:03.780
I know it's coming.
我知道接下来要发生什么了。

373
00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:10.860
Where Mrs. Olsen comes in with a shopping bag, and the housewife says, forget the coffee, just give me the shopping bag.
奥尔森太太提着购物袋走进来，家庭主妇说，别管咖啡了，把购物袋给我就行。

374
00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:12.360
I want to tell you...
我想告诉你们……

375
00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:21.560
Okay, so here's the thing.
好了，事情是这样的。

376
00:16:21.800 --> 00:16:23.580
So, based off that bit...
所以，基于那个段子……

377
00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:33.160
Yeah, it seems sort of innocent, this joke, but I guess there was a gas shortage going on, and he had read an article that there was a commercial toilet paper shortage,
没错，这个玩笑看起来挺无害的，但我猜当时正值汽油短缺，他读到了一篇关于商用卫生纸短缺的文章，

378
00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:36.200
but nothing to do with household toilet paper.
但那跟家用卫生纸完全没关系。

379
00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:45.600
But after people heard this joke, people made a run on the supermarket, and they bought up all the toilet paper and created a toilet paper shortage.
可人们听了这个玩笑后，纷纷涌向超市，抢光了所有的卫生纸，从而引发了一场卫生纸短缺。

380
00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:48.820
And Johnny Carson had to release a statement.
强尼·卡森不得不发表了一份声明。

381
00:16:49.460 --> 00:16:51.420
He said this, quote,
他是这么说的，引用如下：

382
00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:55.680
I don't want to be remembered as the man who created a false toilet paper scare.
我可不想被人们记住是因为我制造了一场虚假的卫生纸恐慌。

383
00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:59.920
I just picked up the item from the paper and enlarged it somewhat.
我只是从报纸上看到了这个消息，然后稍微夸大了一下。

384
00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:02.440
There is no shortage, end quote.
根本没有短缺，引用结束。

385
00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:04.340
I like that that's how he doesn't want to be known.
我喜欢他不想以那种方式被记住这一点。

386
00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:05.260
Yeah, please.
没错，拜托了。

387
00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.200
All right, final thing before we go to break.
好了，进广告前的最后一件事。

388
00:17:09.340 --> 00:17:15.520
Angela, about toilet paper, the hanging of your toilet paper on the roll is a huge source of debate.
安吉拉，关于卫生纸，卫生纸卷挂在架子上的方向一直是个巨大的争议点。

389
00:17:15.880 --> 00:17:19.840
People want to know, should the tail go over or under?
人们想知道，纸头应该朝外还是朝里？

390
00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:20.760
Over.
朝外。

391
00:17:21.080 --> 00:17:21.420
Over.
朝外。

392
00:17:21.620 --> 00:17:21.940
Over.
朝外。

393
00:17:22.120 --> 00:17:23.300
It rolls more naturally.
这样拉起来更顺手。

394
00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:23.760
Over.
朝外。

395
00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:24.940
What debate?
有什么好争议的？

396
00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:25.480
Over.
朝外。

397
00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:34.040
In a survey of Americans, 70% prefer over the top because those 70% of Americans are right.
在一项针对美国人的调查中，70%的人倾向于朝外，因为这70%的美国人是对的。

398
00:17:34.460 --> 00:17:36.040
Yes, you are correct, actually.
没错，事实上你是对的。

399
00:17:36.040 --> 00:17:41.540
There's a patent from 1891 for the Albany Perforated Wrapping Paper Company.
奥尔巴尼穿孔包装纸公司在1891年申请过一项专利。

400
00:17:41.840 --> 00:17:45.940
They filed their patent, which shows it in the overhang fashion, and the patent was approved.
他们提交的专利图纸显示的就是纸头朝外的挂法，而且专利已经获批了。

401
00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:48.720
This is the way it was designed to be used.
这正是它被设计出来的使用方式。

402
00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:49.420
Come on, you guys.
得了吧，你们几个。

403
00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:49.800
Oh, my gosh.
天哪。

404
00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:51.020
From 1891.
始于1891年。

405
00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:53.500
Who's the guy who applied for the patent?
是谁申请的专利？

406
00:17:53.780 --> 00:17:54.540
Sean Wheeler.
肖恩·惠勒。

407
00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:56.200
Sean, thank you.
肖恩，谢谢你。

408
00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:57.380
Thank you for the over.
谢谢你的补充。

409
00:17:57.380 --> 00:18:00.500
For also proving us right.
也谢谢你证明了我们是对的。

410
00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.040
That's all I got for Fast Facts.
这就是我准备的所有冷知识了。

411
00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:05.320
Well, I loved all of that.
嗯，我喜欢这些内容。

412
00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:10.280
Let's take a break because when we come back, there's a moment that Phyllis could not get through.
我们休息一下，回来后，有一个菲利斯怎么也演不下去的片段。

413
00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:12.180
She laughed every time.
她每次都笑场。

414
00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.040
They don't have a take of her not laughing.
他们根本没拍到她不笑的版本。

415
00:18:15.040 --> 00:18:19.220
So what you see was just Phyllis trying not to completely lose it.
所以你看到的只是菲利斯在努力不彻底笑崩。

416
00:18:19.780 --> 00:18:19.960
Yeah.
没错。

417
00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:21.960
But you can see her kind of losing it.
但你能看出她快要憋不住了。

418
00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:22.480
I know.
我知道。

419
00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:24.940
But that was the best one they had.
但这已经是他们拍到的最好的一条了。

420
00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:34.650
We are back.
我们回来了。

421
00:18:34.890 --> 00:18:39.510
And Dwight has decided that he is going to work on the dexterity of his feet.
德怀特决定要锻炼他双脚的灵活性。

422
00:18:39.510 --> 00:18:42.330
He is going to learn to use his feet to do things.
他要学习用脚来做各种事情。

423
00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:47.930
And this is what leads to him trying to drink a cup of coffee with his feet.
这就是导致他尝试用脚喝咖啡的原因。

424
00:18:48.070 --> 00:18:50.130
And he spills it on his crotch.
结果他把咖啡洒在了裤裆上。

425
00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:53.410
You guys, Rain really did spill that on his crotch.
伙计们，雷恩当时是真的把咖啡洒在了裤裆上。

426
00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:55.310
He had to change pants.
他不得不去换裤子。

427
00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:57.130
It was not hot.
咖啡并不烫。

428
00:18:57.930 --> 00:19:00.630
But Phyllis lost it every time.
但菲利斯每次都笑场。

429
00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:04.850
And you can see her covering her mouth with her hands.
你可以看到她用手捂住了嘴。

430
00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:10.690
And we were all supposed to kind of be giggling, I guess, when he spills the coffee.
我想我们当时都应该表现出那种咯咯笑的样子，当他洒了咖啡的时候。

431
00:19:11.170 --> 00:19:13.410
But she's so completely out of character.
但她完全脱离了角色。

432
00:19:13.970 --> 00:19:16.770
I want you guys to look at 55 seconds.
我想让你们看看55秒处。

433
00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:20.570
Did anyone else notice the coffee mug that Dwight is using?
还有人注意到德怀特用的那个咖啡杯吗？

434
00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:21.790
Anyone else out there?
还有其他人吗？

435
00:19:21.970 --> 00:19:28.710
I noticed it because it said Coney Island, which is far from Scranton.
我注意到了，因为它上面写着“康尼岛”，那离斯克兰顿很远。

436
00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:29.750
Okay.
好吧。

437
00:19:29.750 --> 00:19:32.330
Are you ready for a little mom detective journey?
准备好来一场“妈妈侦探”之旅了吗？

438
00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:32.890
Please.
请讲。

439
00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:33.350
Okay.
好的。

440
00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:38.370
So I went online to see if I could find the exact one that Dwight is using in this scene.
我上网查了一下，想看看能不能找到德怀特在这一幕中用的那个同款杯子。

441
00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:42.430
And Jenna, I remembered that when we broke down the Sabre episode,
珍娜，我记得当我们拆解“赛博”那一集时，

442
00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:46.350
you had done some digging on hot dog eateries in Pennsylvania.
你曾对宾夕法尼亚州的热狗店做过一些调查。

443
00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:46.910
Yeah.
没错。

444
00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.330
Because Dwight has that great scene where he's holding the tray of hot dogs
因为德怀特有个很棒的场景，他端着一盘热狗，

445
00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:53.010
and he doesn't know where to put them when he meets Gabe.
在见到盖博时不知道该把它们放在哪儿。

446
00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:54.250
All right.
好的。

447
00:19:54.450 --> 00:20:00.590
Well, you found an article in Pennsylvania Eats about a local spot in Scranton called Coney Island Lunch.
你当时在《宾夕法尼亚美食》上找到了一篇关于斯克兰顿当地一家叫“康尼岛午餐”店的文章。

448
00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:02.550
And I was like, oh, that's got to be it.
我当时就想，噢，肯定就是它了。

449
00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:03.110
Right.
没错。

450
00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:04.450
They serve Texas wieners.
他们供应德州风味热狗。

451
00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:05.310
This has got to be it.
肯定就是这个了。

452
00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:10.330
But Dwight's mug said Coney Island, Texas lunch.
但德怀特的杯子上写的是“康尼岛德州午餐”。

453
00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:14.190
So I started Googling because we are lit as a Google.
所以我开始用谷歌搜索，因为我们可是谷歌达人。

454
00:20:14.670 --> 00:20:16.710
And I found two different restaurants.
结果我发现了两家不同的餐馆。

455
00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:20.370
They had almost identical names, but two different storefronts.
它们的名字几乎一模一样，但店面却完全不同。

456
00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.190
And I thought, well, maybe I've got this wrong.
我当时想，好吧，也许是我搞错了。

457
00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:22.830
Right.
没错。

458
00:20:22.830 --> 00:20:23.050
Okay.
好的。

459
00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:26.890
There's two places in Scranton called Coney Island something?
斯克兰顿竟然有两家店都叫“康尼岛”什么的？

460
00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:27.630
Yeah.
是的。

461
00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:29.450
So I was like, this has got to be wrong.
所以我心想，这肯定不对劲。

462
00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.070
So I dug a little deeper.
于是我深入挖掘了一下。

463
00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:36.310
And then I came across this delightful blogger who writes about food as he travels.
然后我偶然发现了一位有趣的博主，他会在旅行时写写美食。

464
00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:36.850
Okay.
好的。

465
00:20:37.190 --> 00:20:40.790
I only know him by his blogger handle, Hack Knife.
我只知道他的博主昵称叫“黑客刀”。

466
00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:41.390
Okay.
好的。

467
00:20:41.630 --> 00:20:44.550
So Hack Knife, if you're out there, this delighted me.
所以“黑客刀”，如果你能看到的话，这真的让我很开心。

468
00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:50.030
He was traveling to Ohio with his family over Thanksgiving a few years back.
几年前感恩节期间，他带着家人去俄亥俄州旅行。

469
00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:52.890
And he made a pit stop in Scranton for lunch.
途中他在斯克兰顿停下来吃午饭。

470
00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:54.610
And here's what he had to say.
以下是他当时写的内容。

471
00:20:54.830 --> 00:21:00.170
We walked up Lackawanna Avenue to find two very similar sounding restaurants located just
我们沿着拉卡瓦纳大道走去，发现两家名字非常相似的餐馆，它们的位置

472
00:21:00.170 --> 00:21:01.690
a mere steps from each other.
仅仅相隔几步之遥。

473
00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:05.150
Coney Island, Texas lunch at 100 Cedar Ave.
一家是位于雪松大道100号的“康尼岛德州午餐”。

474
00:21:05.150 --> 00:21:09.270
And Coney Island lunch at 515 Lackawanna Ave.
另一家是位于拉卡瓦纳大道515号的“康尼岛午餐”。

475
00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:09.730
Okay, wait.
等等，等等。

476
00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:13.290
So the only thing that's different is one of them is called Texas lunch and one of them
所以唯一的区别就是其中一家叫“德州午餐”，而另一家

477
00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:14.070
is called lunch.
只叫“午餐”。

478
00:21:14.310 --> 00:21:15.170
That's right.
没错。

479
00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:15.690
Okay.
好吧。

